      subtitle: 'Styled at a Year 5 level across the same three multiple-choice domains as the real test — Reading, Conventions of Language and Numeracy — plus two bonus Writing prompts. Answers are at the end.',
      passage: {
        title: 'Reading passage: "Bringing Back the Bettong"',
        text: "Fifty years ago, the eastern bettong disappeared from mainland Australia. This small, hopping marsupial — about the size of a rabbit — once played an important role in the bush. As it dug for roots, fungi and insects, it turned over the soil, letting water and nutrients sink in and helping native seeds sprout.\n\nWhen foxes and habitat loss pushed the bettong to extinction on the mainland, a small population survived on the island of Tasmania, where there are no foxes.\n\nIn 2012, scientists began an ambitious project: catching bettongs in Tasmania and releasing them into a fox-free sanctuary west of Canberra. Cameras were set up to monitor how the animals settled in. Within a few years, the released bettongs were breeding successfully, and soil samples showed the ground was healthier in areas where they had been digging.\n\nToday, similar rewilding projects are being planned across the country, giving conservationists hope that other lost mainland species might one day return.",
      },
      questions: [
        { num: 1, subject: 'Reading', prompt: 'According to the passage, what is the eastern bettong most similar in size to?', options: ['A cat', 'A rabbit', 'A fox', 'A wombat'], answer: 1, explanation: 'The passage states the bettong is "about the size of a rabbit."' },
        { num: 2, subject: 'Reading', prompt: 'What did bettongs do as they searched for food underground?', options: ['Built nests', 'Turned over the soil', 'Chased insects above ground', 'Ate native seeds directly from trees'], answer: 1, explanation: 'The text says digging for food "turned over the soil, letting water and nutrients sink in."' },
        { num: 3, subject: 'Reading', prompt: 'Why did the eastern bettong disappear from mainland Australia?', options: ['Bushfires destroyed their habitat', 'Farmers hunted them for food', 'Foxes and loss of habitat', 'A disease spread among them'], answer: 2, explanation: 'The passage directly names "foxes and habitat loss" as the cause.' },
        { num: 4, subject: 'Reading', prompt: 'Where did a population of eastern bettongs survive?', options: ['New Zealand', 'Tasmania', 'Western Australia', 'A wildlife park in Sydney'], answer: 1, explanation: 'The passage states a population "survived on the island of Tasmania."' },
        { num: 5, subject: 'Reading', prompt: 'What was special about the sanctuary west of Canberra where the bettongs were released?', options: ['It had no foxes', 'It had lots of tourists', 'It was next to a river', 'It had been recently burnt'], answer: 0, explanation: 'The sanctuary is described as "fox-free."' },
        { num: 6, subject: 'Reading', prompt: 'What evidence suggests the released bettongs were helping the environment?', options: ['Tourist numbers increased', 'Soil samples showed healthier ground', 'Fox numbers decreased', 'The sanctuary grew larger'], answer: 1, explanation: 'The passage says "soil samples showed the ground was healthier" where the bettongs had been digging — this is an inferential question, testing whether the reader can connect that detail back to the animal\'s role described earlier.' },
        { num: 7, subject: 'Reading', prompt: 'What is the passage mainly about?', options: ['The dangers foxes pose to native wildlife', 'A successful project to reintroduce a lost species', 'How to dig for roots and fungi', "The history of Tasmania's wildlife parks"], answer: 1, explanation: 'The passage as a whole describes the reintroduction project and its early success — this is a main-idea question, not a single-detail one.' },
        { num: 8, subject: 'Numeracy', prompt: 'What is 3/4 written as a decimal?', options: ['0.34', '0.75', '1.34', '3.4'], answer: 1, explanation: '3 ÷ 4 = 0.75.' },
        { num: 9, subject: 'Numeracy', prompt: 'A rectangle has a length of 9 cm and a width of 4 cm. What is its area?', options: ['13 cm²', '26 cm²', '36 cm²', '45 cm²'], answer: 2, explanation: 'Area = length × width = 9 × 4 = 36 cm².' },
        { num: 10, subject: 'Numeracy', prompt: 'Look at the pattern: 4, 9, 14, 19, ... What is the next number?', options: ['22', '23', '24', '25'], answer: 2, explanation: 'The pattern increases by 5 each time, so 19 + 5 = 24.' },
        { num: 11, subject: 'Numeracy', prompt: 'Maya starts her homework at 4:15 pm and finishes 50 minutes later. What time does she finish?', options: ['4:65 pm', '5:05 pm', '5:00 pm', '5:15 pm'], answer: 1, explanation: '4:15 pm + 50 minutes = 5:05 pm.' },
        { num: 12, subject: 'Numeracy', prompt: 'Which fraction is equivalent to 2/6?', options: ['1/2', '1/3', '2/3', '3/6'], answer: 1, explanation: '2/6 simplifies to 1/3 (divide both by 2).' },
        { num: 13, subject: 'Numeracy', prompt: 'A survey of 30 students found 12 prefer soccer and 10 prefer basketball. The rest prefer swimming. How many prefer swimming?', options: ['6', '8', '18', '22'], answer: 1, explanation: '30 − 12 − 10 = 8.' },
        { num: 14, subject: 'Numeracy', prompt: 'What is 456 rounded to the nearest hundred?', options: ['400', '450', '500', '460'], answer: 2, explanation: '456 is closer to 500 than 400, since the tens digit (5) rounds up.' },
        { num: 15, subject: 'Conventions of Language', prompt: 'Which word is spelled correctly?', options: ['Recieve', 'Receive', 'Receve', 'Receeve'], answer: 1, explanation: '"Receive" follows the "i before e except after c" pattern.' },
        { num: 16, subject: 'Conventions of Language', prompt: 'Which word is spelled correctly?', options: ['Definitly', 'Definately', 'Definitely', 'Deffinitely'], answer: 2, explanation: '"Definitely" — a commonly misspelled word; remember it contains "finite."' },
        { num: 17, subject: 'Conventions of Language', prompt: 'Choose the correct sentence.', options: ['Yesterday, we goes to the beach.', 'Yesterday, we went to the beach.', 'Yesterday, we go to the beach.', 'Yesterday, we going to the beach.'], answer: 1, explanation: '"Yesterday" signals past tense, so "went" is the correct verb form.' },
        { num: 18, subject: 'Conventions of Language', prompt: 'Which sentence uses punctuation correctly?', options: ['My favourite subjects are, maths reading and art.', 'My favourite subjects are maths, reading, and art.', 'My favourite subjects are: maths reading and art.', 'My favourite subjects, are maths reading and art.'], answer: 1, explanation: 'Items in a list need commas between them: "maths, reading, and art."' },
        { num: 19, subject: 'Conventions of Language', prompt: "Choose the correct word to complete the sentence: 'The dog wagged ___ tail happily.'", options: ["it's", 'its', "its'", 'their'], answer: 1, explanation: '"Its" (no apostrophe) is the possessive form; "it\'s" always means "it is."' },
        { num: 20, subject: 'Conventions of Language', prompt: 'Choose the correct sentence.', options: ['The group of students were late.', 'The group of students was late.', 'The group of students is were late.', 'The group of student were late.'], answer: 1, explanation: '"Group" is a singular collective noun, so it takes the singular verb "was."' },
      ],
