        text: 'For years, the two halves of the growing city had been separated by a wide, deep bay. Ferries carried workers and families across the water every morning, but the boats were slow, and on windy days the crossing could take twice as long. The city council finally agreed: it was time to build a bridge.\n\nLeading the project was an engineer named Maria Chen, who had spent months studying the bay before a single beam was ever lifted. The riverbed beneath the water was soft and muddy in places, which meant the foundations for the bridge\'s towers had to be sunk unusually deep to find solid rock to rest on. Large ships also needed to pass beneath the bridge, so the roadway would have to be held high above the water by two tall towers and thick steel cables, in a design known as a suspension bridge. To speed up construction, Maria\'s team built the roadway outward from both towers at once, section by section, until the two halves finally met in the middle above the deepest part of the bay.\n\nBefore any real steel was cut, the engineers built a scale model of the bridge and tested it inside a wind tunnel, checking that it would not sway or twist too much in a storm. Only once the model passed every test did construction on the real bridge begin. Three years later, crowds gathered as the first cars crossed. The bridge cut the journey between the two halves of the city from nearly an hour to under ten minutes, and it soon became a well-known landmark — not just for its size, but as a reminder of what careful planning, patient testing and teamwork could achieve.',
