        title: 'Reading passage: "The Robot in the Library"',
        text: "Mrs Okafor unlocked the library doors at half past eight, the way she had every morning for eleven years. This morning, though, something new was waiting by the returns desk: a robot named Circe.\n\nCirce was about the height of a Year 4 student, with a round screen for a face and wheels instead of feet. The council had sent her to the library for a six-month trial, to see whether a robot could help students find books, sort returns and answer simple questions during the busiest lunchtimes.\n\nAt first, most students kept their distance. A few worried that Circe might replace Mrs Okafor altogether, or that she would make mistakes shelving books in the wrong order. Amir, a quiet boy from Year 5, was the first to test her properly. He asked Circe to recommend a book about volcanoes, and within seconds she wheeled to the science shelf and pointed a small light at exactly the right spot.\n\nBy the second week, students had grown used to Circe's presence. She could not tell jokes as well as Mrs Okafor, and she sometimes paused for a long moment before answering, as if thinking hard. But she never lost patience with the same question asked twenty times in an hour, and she kept a perfectly tidy record of which books were most borrowed.\n\nMrs Okafor noticed something else: since Circe arrived, more students were visiting the library during lunch, not fewer. Some came out of curiosity, but many stayed to read. When the council's six-month trial ended, they asked the students to vote on whether Circe should stay permanently. The vote was almost unanimous. Circe, it seemed, had not replaced anyone — she had simply given Mrs Okafor more time to talk with students about the books they loved.",
